# Normalize on commit, check out with LF everywhere.
#
# This repository is byte-sensitive in ways most are not:
#
#   * `qqq-pkg::store` compares content digests, and `qqq-lock`/`qqq.lock`
#     carries a covering hash over its own bytes. A file that changes line
#     endings between checkout and commit produces a different digest for
#     the same logical content.
#   * fixtures and expected-output files are asserted against exactly.
#   * the reproducibility claim (REL/REP items in the Checklist) is only
#     meaningful if the bytes on disk are the bytes that were committed.
#
# `core.autocrlf` must be `false` in this repository, and `.gitattributes`
# alone does NOT achieve that. Measured, not assumed:
#
#   With `core.autocrlf=true` and `eol=lf` here, `git ls-files --eol` reported
#   `i/lf w/crlf` for every Markdown document. The attributes control what is
#   *committed*; `autocrlf` controls what Git writes to the *working tree* on
#   checkout and on index refresh. The two are independent, and where they
#   disagree the working tree drifts every time Git touches a file.
#
# The symptom is a tree that reports files modified after a commit that included
# them, with an empty `git diff` — and a dirty tree hides real changes. A
# previous version of this comment claimed `eol=lf` "overrides" `autocrlf`; it
# does not, and the drift recurred until the config was set.
#
# The fix is per-repository configuration, which is why it cannot live in this
# file:
#
#     git config core.autocrlf false
#
# `tools/normalize_eol.py --check` runs in CI and fails if a tracked text file
# would check out with the wrong endings, so a regression is caught rather than
# rediscovered.
#
# The default: everything is text and gets LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary formats must never be touched. `text=auto` already detects most,
# but these three are called out because they are load-bearing:
#   - `.wasm`/`.cwasm` are compiled artifacts whose digests are compared.
#   - `.wit` is text, but it is listed as text on purpose for diffs.
*.wasm  binary
*.cwasm binary
*.wat   text eol=lf
*.wit   text eol=lf

# Vendored/lock data that must round-trip exactly.
*.lock  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf

# Scripts: LF, so they behave the same in CI containers as locally.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf

# Windows-native files keep CRLF, because the tooling that reads them
# (PowerShell, cmd) is inconsistent about bare LF in some contexts.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf

# Images and archives.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
